    kind: invariant  # 6d9c
    title: "Dataflow annotations destroyed by edge.meta overwrite in 4 polyglot linkers"  # 6d9c
    status: violated  # 6d9c
    priority: 1  # 6d9c
    parent:  # 6d9c
    tags:  # 6d9c
    - analysis_quality  # 6d9c
    - cross_language_linkers  # 6d9c
    - dataflow  # 6d9c
    before: []  # 6d9c
    duplicate_of: []  # 6d9c
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 6d9c
    pr_ref:  # 6d9c
    description: "Discovered during WI-hukoh Phase A telemetry baseline data collection: four cross-language linkers silently destroy the dataflow annotations (`access_mode`, `dest_access_mode`, `channel`) that they pass to `Edge.create` at construction time.\n\n## How the bug works\n\n`Edge.create` at `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/ir.py:379-436` accepts `access_mode`, `dest_access_mode`, and `channel` as kwargs and merges them into `edge.meta` (lines 402-416). The result is an edge whose `meta` dict contains the dataflow annotation the linker requested.\n\nFour linkers then immediately overwrite the just-populated `edge.meta` with a fresh dict that contains linker-specific metadata but NOT the dataflow fields:\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/linkers/event_sourcing.py:750-770` — 1 site\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/linkers/ipc.py:529-545` — message_send edges\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/linkers/ipc.py:562-578` — message_receive edges\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/linkers/websocket.py:572-585+` — websocket_message edges\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/linkers/message_queue.py:500-513+` — message_queue edges\n\nPattern in all 4:\n```python\nedge = Edge.create(\n    src=..., dst=...,\n    access_mode=\"write\",\n    dest_access_mode=\"read\",\n    channel=channel_name,\n)\nedge.meta = {\n    \"channel\": channel_name,\n    \"channel_type\": ...,\n    # No access_mode, no dest_access_mode — these are now lost.\n}\n```\n\n## Evidence\n\nOn a buildkit behavior map (42,792 total edges, 21,012 with `access_mode`, 949 `event-sourcing-linker-v1` edges):\n\n```\njq '[.edges[] | select(.meta.dest_access_mode != null)] | length' buildkit.json\n0\n```\n\nZero edges with `dest_access_mode` populated anywhere in the entire repo's behavior map, despite 949 event-sourcing edges and likely IPC/websocket/message_queue edges existing.\n\nOn alertmanager (smaller Go repo): also 0 edges with `dest_access_mode`.\n\n## Impact\n\n1. **WI-hukoh Phase C decision gate is unmeasurable until this is fixed.** The `would_admit_dst_reader` telemetry counter (Phase A instrumentation) reports 0 on every repo because there are no edges with `dest_access_mode` to count. Without real data, we cannot decide whether option 2 (dst_mode OR-check in slice BFS) is valuable.\n\n2. **The 16-linker \"schema-anticipated option 2\" claim was wrong.** In my earlier WI-hukoh analysis I stated that 16 cross-language linkers populate `dest_access_mode` and that the schema \"anticipates\" option 2. The call-site count is accurate — 16 linkers DO call `Edge.create(..., dest_access_mode=...)`. But 4 of the 16 immediately destroy the field before the edge is persisted. The schema-anticipated story is 12 linkers, not 16. (Still the right direction, but weaker evidence.)\n\n3. **Forward dataflow slicing has been under-covering these 4 linker edge types.** Every event_publishes, message_send, message_receive, websocket_message, and message_queue edge has been admitted to dataflow slices only via the \"graceful degradation — no access_mode\" path in slice.py BFS. That path admits them unconditionally, so the slice isn't missing edges — but the slice is not distinguishing writer-from-reader semantics for these cross-language boundaries, which means the \"data flows OUT\" story from ADR-0015 §6 is weaker at cross-language boundaries than at intra-language boundaries.\n\n4. **Any tier ranking or centrality metric that reads edge.meta.access_mode gets a lopsided signal** — intra-language edges are annotated, cross-language boundary edges are not, so centrality on cross-language nodes is systematically lower than it should be. Severity depends on which consumers actually use access_mode (not yet audited).\n\n## Violated invariant\n\n**INVARIANT: Dataflow annotations set via `Edge.create(access_mode=, dest_access_mode=, channel=)` kwargs persist into the final edge's `meta` dict and are not silently overwritten by subsequent assignments.**\n\nCorollary: linkers that need to set both dataflow annotations AND linker-specific metadata must pass both via a single `Edge.create(meta={...})` kwarg, letting `Edge.create` merge them. The current pattern of `edge = Edge.create(access_mode=...); edge.meta = {...}` is structurally broken.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nFor each of the 4 linkers: rewrite the `edge.meta = {...}` assignment to instead pass the linker metadata as a `meta={...}` kwarg to `Edge.create`. `Edge.create` already merges the `meta` kwarg with the dataflow kwargs (ir.py:411-414), so both end up in the final edge.\n\nExample for event_sourcing.py:\n```python\n# Before (BROKEN):\nedge = Edge.create(\n    ...\n    access_mode=\"write\",\n    dest_access_mode=\"read\",\n    channel=publisher.event_name,\n)\nedge.meta = {\n    \"event_name\": publisher.event_name,\n    \"publisher_framework\": publisher.framework,\n    ...\n}\n\n# After (CORRECT):\nedge = Edge.create(\n    ...\n    access_mode=\"write\",\n    dest_access_mode=\"read\",\n    channel=publisher.event_name,\n    meta={\n        \"event_name\": publisher.event_name,\n        \"publisher_framework\": publisher.framework,\n        ...\n    },\n)\n```\n\nSame shape for the 4 other sites (2 in ipc.py, 1 in websocket.py, 1 in message_queue.py).\n\n## Regression test\n\nAdd a fixture-level test per linker: run the linker on a minimal fixture, verify the output edges have `access_mode` and `dest_access_mode` populated. These should have been present from the start; their absence is why the bug was hidden.\n\n## Why this was hidden\n\n`Edge.create` does not fail loudly when its dataflow kwargs are overwritten. It accepts them, merges them, and returns the edge — silently. The subsequent `edge.meta = {...}` assignment is legal Python and has no runtime warning. The only signal of the bug is downstream: slice telemetry shows 0 `dest_access_mode` edges, but until WI-hukoh Phase A added that telemetry, there was no observability into per-rule admission counts. Pre-telemetry, the symptom (slices are slightly smaller than they could be at cross-language boundaries) was invisible.\n\n## Dependencies / ordering\n\n- MUST fix this before WI-hukoh Phase B (option 2 implementation) so the decision gate in Phase C has real data.\n- INV-sabum is the earlier \"propagating access_mode at the wrong layer\" invariant. This bug is the opposite shape — dataflow fields set at the RIGHT layer (Edge.create), destroyed by linker code that didn't know to preserve them. The two invariants should reinforce each other in the ADR documentation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- 4 linker files (event_sourcing, ipc, websocket, message_queue) — ~20 lines of change each.\n- New regression tests — ~50 lines per linker.\n- CHANGELOG entry.\n- NOT governance-protected — linker code is tier-1 source, no human approval needed.\n\nPrecursor PR to WI-hukoh Phase B. Immediate work."  # 6d9c
    fields:  # 6d9c
      statement: "Dataflow annotations (access_mode, dest_access_mode, channel) set via Edge.create kwargs persist into the final edge's meta dict and are not silently overwritten by subsequent edge.meta assignments in linker code."  # 6d9c
      root_cause: "Four linkers (event_sourcing, ipc, websocket, message_queue) follow the pattern 'edge = Edge.create(access_mode=...); edge.meta = {...}' where the second line overwrites the first line's merged meta. Edge.create accepts a meta= kwarg that would merge linker metadata with dataflow fields, but the linkers don't use it."  # 6d9c
